sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch08-14> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Pollen grains and spores formed as tetrahedral tetrads keep the plane facets and sharp angles of the tetrad if their walls solidify before the grains separate, but become spherical if they separate while still soft.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Pollen-grain shape depends on whether walls harden before the tetrad separates";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter VIII, “The Forms of Tissues, or Cell-Aggregates (continued)”; topic “Tetrads of pollen and spores”. Scope: pollen grains of angiosperms and spores of ferns and red algae formed in tetrads"
}
